(3765898, 532227, 66515, '<td valign="top">I like the shot but I''m not a fan of the border. It kinda stops the eye and then lets it go for a second before its stopped by the real border. I had a quick peek at this without the border (just dropped it into PSP and removed the border just to look) and I like it a lot better without it.</td>', 'I like the shot but I''m not a fan of the border. It kinda stops the eye and then lets it go for a second before its stopped by the real border. I had a quick peek at this without the border (just dropped it into PSP and removed the border just to look) and I like it a lot better without it.', '2007-06-11 02:08:46.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765903, 531228, 66709, '<td valign="top">This shot reminds me of Taxi the show the finaly end the producer walking away   Good shot</td>', 'This shot reminds me of Taxi the show the finaly end the producer walking away   Good shot', '2007-06-11 02:10:40.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765909, 532865, 7252, '<td valign="top">Would be better with a tighter crop on top and without the green hue.</td>', 'Would be better with a tighter crop on top and without the green hue.', '2007-06-11 02:13:13.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765920, 532853, 66515, '<td valign="top">A few areas to improve on with this shot. The focus is a little off for starters. It appears you used a fairly small aperture as the people in the background are still quite visible. With a larger aperture they would just about disappear. (I''m curious what apertures you had available)<br/> <br/>The shadow on his face is quite dark. Yes it was probably a bright sunny day, but flash would have brightened up his face so you could actually see it. <br/> <br/>This also has a snapshot feeling to it. The difference between a candid and a snapshot is subtle. A snapshot is generally where you more or less aim the camera and hit the button without really thinking about the shot. Its more or less than photographic equivalent to "shooting from the hip". Sometimes you hit but more often than not you miss. <br/> <br/>In a candid you capture a moment: What I would describe as "people being people".  This feeling really quite posed and staged. If you captured him just after shanking the ball or making a tough putt that would have been more "real". I took about 3 or 4 shots for this challenge and while the people in the shot were aware of being photographed, I instructed to not actually pay any attention to me. I told them to just keep on doing what they were doing. While they aren''t ribbon winners you''ll find when you look through the photos in this challenge, the best ones are the ones that just capture the moment with staging it. <br/> <br/>That aside? I like the colors in this shot. Your colors are nicely saturated. It looks like the person you photographed was an interesting subject. A bit of a character as it were. In that respect you did quite well and managed to capture his "essence".</td>', 'A few areas to improve on with this shot. The focus is a little off for starters. It appears you used a fairly small aperture as the people in the background are still quite visible. With a larger aperture they would just about disappear. (I''m curious what apertures you had available) The shadow on his face is quite dark. Yes it was probably a bright sunny day, but flash would have brightened up his face so you could actually see it.  This also has a snapshot feeling to it. The difference between a candid and a snapshot is subtle. A snapshot is generally where you more or less aim the camera and hit the button without really thinking about the shot. Its more or less than photographic equivalent to "shooting from the hip". Sometimes you hit but more often than not you miss.  In a candid you capture a moment: What I would describe as "people being people".  This feeling really quite posed and staged. If you captured him just after shanking the ball or making a tough putt that would have been more "real". I took about 3 or 4 shots for this challenge and while the people in the shot were aware of being photographed, I instructed to not actually pay any attention to me. I told them to just keep on doing what they were doing. While they aren''t ribbon winners you''ll find when you look through the photos in this challenge, the best ones are the ones that just capture the moment with staging it.  That aside? I like the colors in this shot. Your colors are nicely saturated. It looks like the person you photographed was an interesting subject. A bit of a character as it were. In that respect you did quite well and managed to capture his "essence".', '2007-06-11 02:20:46.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765924, 532524, 66515, '<td valign="top">Interesting shot but the post processing really doesn''t do the subject justice and really isn''t flattering.</td>', 'Interesting shot but the post processing really doesn''t do the subject justice and really isn''t flattering.', '2007-06-11 02:22:30.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765925, 532806, 60195, '<td valign="top">Almost didn''t catch it. I actually wasn;t voting yet, and clicked on past this, but immediately hit the BACK button.  That is subtle... I LOVE this !  Nice job on highlighting her face only. What a perfect look. I just hope everyone takes the time to see this. 10 from me. Well done.</td>', 'Almost didn''t catch it. I actually wasn;t voting yet, and clicked on past this, but immediately hit the BACK button.  That is subtle... I LOVE this !  Nice job on highlighting her face only. What a perfect look. I just hope everyone takes the time to see this. 10 from me. Well done.', '2007-06-11 02:23:08.000000', NULL, 1),
(3765929, 532772, 66515, '<td valign="top">If I were to say what this picture is about I would say its about the canal and the water. The person just seems to be an extra element that kinda walked into the scene. Composing the shot to put more emphasis on the person would definitely help. For me the shot really ends at the top of the wall because the archway is a such a strong visual element it keeps drawing my eye towards it.</td>', 'If I were to say what this picture is about I would say its about the canal and the water. The person just seems to be an extra element that kinda walked into the scene. Composing the shot to put more emphasis on the person would definitely help. For me the shot really ends at the top of the wall because the archway is a such a strong visual element it keeps drawing my eye towards it.', '2007-06-11 02:25:29.000000', NULL, 1),
